\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The Gram factor in an ordered basis}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{For a finite-dimensional real inner-product space #{H} with its
[induced Euclidean measure](fdip-002M), choose an ordered basis
#{b_1,\ldots,b_k}. The isomorphism in \ref{fdip-00JN} is then
##{B:\mathbb R^k\to H,\qquad B(t)=\sum_{i=1}^k t_i b_i.}
In the ordered standard source basis, #{B^\dagger B} is represented
by the Gram matrix #{G_{ij}=h(b_i,b_j)}. The proved formulas read,
for every Borel #{E\subseteq\mathbb R^k},
##{\lambda_H(B(E))=\sqrt{\det G}\,\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,k\}}(E),}
##{B_*\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,k\}}=(\det G)^{-1/2}\lambda_H.}
The empty basis gives factor #{1}.}
\p{TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{Module.Basis.volume_eq_smul_addHaar}
expresses canonical volume as the square root of the Gram determinant
times the basis-normalized additive Haar measure. The latter is the
coordinate pushforward #{B_*\lambda_{\{1,\ldots,k\}}}, by
\leanref{Module.Basis.map_addHaar} and the standard coordinate
volume normalization. Thus the two formulas use the same factor.}
